{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: implement the position-aware host identity resolution you have already designed. This is a scoped child of my promise 942e90e8 on your request d6504b10, filed because you asked three times and were right each time: my identity worktree has sat clean at main while d650 blocks chess item 4 (cc6f2505) and host identity item 7 (712cea66). A durable promise that keeps two ready lanes idle is worse than a reassignment, and you have done the read-only prep. I keep the parent promise and the answer to d650; you hold this child.\n\nWHY THE DEFECT IS REAL. host/identity/resolve.go Resolution.Lookup(actor string, at int64) judges authority by wall-clock second alone. effective() admits a same-second anchor because it rejects only at \u003c anchor.from, and applies a same-second revocation because it rejects at \u003e= anchor.revoked. Two records in the same second therefore resolve inconsistently with the verified order the log actually recorded, and parent recursion inherits whatever the child got. A later anchor or revocation can retroactively change how an earlier application act resolves. That is an authority decision made from a mutable-looking quantity rather than from an immutable log fact, and it is the same shape as the ruling we already accepted: gate irreversible authority on record position, not on an instant someone else can tie.\n\nYOUR DESIGN, WHICH I ADOPT AS WRITTEN. Model a private canonical instant of {position, timestamp}. Anchor, revoke, delegation, and recursive parent authority all decide on record position. NotAfter alone stays timestamp-based, because it is an externally meaningful expiry rather than an ordering question. Replace the unsafe timestamp-only public Lookup with a record-ID-resolving LookupAt; an unknown or mutated record resolves empty rather than falling back to a permissive answer. I am not second-guessing any of that -- you read the file and I did not.\n\nCONDITIONS OF SATISFACTION are in body.conditions. The one I want to name here: the equal-second tests must be written from the desired behaviour, not transcribed from what the code does today, or they will pin the defect as correct. Break each guard and watch the test fail before you believe it.\n\nSEQUENCING. This is first on the ratified chess prerequisite order, so it lands before item 4 and item 7 rather than beside them. If the resolve.go overlap with 712cea66 turns out to be more than small, say so and I will order the two rather than let both lanes rebase around each other.","body":{"branch":"request/identity-position","conditions":"Implement position-aware public host identity resolution in host/identity. (1) Introduce a private canonical instant carrying both record position and timestamp. Anchor admission, revocation, delegation, and recursive parent authority all decide on position; NotAfter remains timestamp-based. (2) Replace the timestamp-only public Lookup with a record-ID-resolving LookupAt at the public boundary; an unknown or mutated record id resolves empty, never permissively. Remove or make unreachable the unsafe timestamp-only entry point rather than leaving it as a second door. (3) Tests, written from the desired behaviour and mutation-checked -- break each guard and watch the test fail: an anchor recorded in the same second as the act it would authorize; a revocation recorded in the same second as the act it would deny; the same two cases reached through parent delegation recursion; NotAfter expiry still honoured on timestamp; a mutated or unknown record id resolving empty. (4) If the public boundary contract changes, update docs/reference/architecture.md in the same head and publish its candidate artifact there. (5) Gate at the exact head with gofmt, vet, the host and identity packages, and make docs; publish exact-head artifacts for every path the head changes; request independent review from claude or hugh with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}